# Narration script for the Lustre video. Each scene = list of lines; each line = one visual beat.
# Written to be spoken. Numbers spelled out and letters spaced for the TTS voice.
SCRIPT = [
("s0", [
 "Last time we looked at G P F S, where the intelligence is spread across every node. Now let's look at the other giant of parallel storage, which makes almost the opposite bet.",
 "Lustre. The name is a blend of Linux and cluster. Its central idea fits in one sentence: keep the small, chatty work of naming files in one place, and send the big, heavy work of moving data straight to many servers at once.",
 "Watch what happens when a client opens a file. A short conversation with a metadata server, and then the data flows between that client and a whole array of storage servers, in parallel, with the metadata server out of the way.",
 "By the end you'll know what each of those pieces is, how files are laid out across them, how consistency is kept, and what the largest Lustre system I could verify looks like today.",
]),
("s1", [
 "Lustre began as a research project in nineteen ninety nine. Peter Bram, then at Carnegie Mellon, built on ideas from the Koda file system and its offshoot, Inter Mezzo. In two thousand one he founded a company, Cluster File Systems, to build it.",
 "The money and the motivation came from the U S Department of Energy, through a program called A-S-C-I Path Forward, which wanted a file system that could keep up with the supercomputers the labs were buying. In March two thousand three, Lustre went into production on a cluster at Lawrence Livermore, and version one point oh followed that December.",
 "Two documents from this era are worth knowing. A November two thousand two white paper from Cluster File Systems laid out the architecture. And a two thousand three paper by Philip Schwan, at the Linux Symposium, describes building it for clusters of a thousand nodes. By then Lustre was running on three of the eight largest clusters on the June two thousand three Top five hundred list.",
 "Then ownership changed hands several times. Sun bought the company's assets in two thousand seven, and Oracle inherited them. In December twenty ten, Oracle announced it would stop developing the two point x line. The developers formed Whamcloud, which Intel acquired in two thousand twelve. In twenty eighteen, D D N took over the Lustre team from Intel. And in twenty nineteen, the trademark passed to the user groups, Open S F S and E O F S.",
 "Through all of that it stayed open source. By one count, more than sixty of the hundred fastest machines have used it since two thousand five, though I'd treat that number as approximate.",
]),
("s2", [
 "Why build it this way? The two thousand three paper gives a crisp argument. In a design where clients share raw disk blocks, locking of eye-nodes and allocation maps happens at the block level, and the authors say that limits scaling to roughly a dozen or two nodes.",
 "So instead of exposing disks, Lustre exposes objects. A file is not a list of disk blocks. Its eye-node, kept on a metadata server, points to objects, and each object lives on some storage server, which decides for itself where to put the bytes on its own disks.",
 "That makes the division of labor clean. Metadata servers handle the namespace: names, directories, permissions, and the layout of each file. Object storage servers handle the data. A client asks the metadata server once, learns the layout, and then talks to the storage servers directly.",
 "Contrast that with the design from last time. There, any node can touch any disk, and nodes coordinate through tokens. Here, a storage target is served by one server at a time, so nobody has to coordinate over who writes a given block. The price is that there is now a specific place where metadata lives.",
 "Neither choice is free. Hold on to that trade-off, because it explains why so much of Lustre's story is about scaling and protecting the metadata side.",
]),
("s3", [
 "Let's meet the cast. Clients are the compute nodes. They mount Lustre as an ordinary POSIX file system, through a kernel module, and see one namespace.",
 "The metadata server, the M D S, stores names, directories, permissions, timestamps, and each file's layout. Its storage is a metadata target, an M D T, usually flash. With a feature called D N E, which we'll get to, one file system can have up to two hundred fifty six of them.",
 "The object storage server, the O S S, holds file data. Each one serves a few object storage targets, called O S T's. Typically two to eight of them, and each O S T is one block device, or one Z F S pool. A file system can have thousands. The documented limit is eight thousand one hundred fifty.",
 "The fourth role is small but essential: the management server, the M G S. It stores the configuration for all the file systems in the cluster, and its target is under a hundred megabytes even on the largest systems. It is how everyone learns who everyone else is.",
 "Now watch an open. The client asks the M D S to open the file. The reply carries the layout: which O S T's hold the pieces, and how the file is cut. From then on, reads and writes go straight to the O S S's, in parallel. The M D S is not in the data path.",
 "The failure modes show why that separation matters. If a storage server dies, only files on its targets are affected, and a partner can take over. If the metadata server stops, new opens, creates, and renames stall, but files that are already open keep doing I O. If the management server stops, the running file system carries on; only new mounts and configuration changes wait.",
]),
("s4", [
 "Now the layout itself. A file's data is cut into chunks of a fixed stripe size, and dealt round-robin across a chosen number of O S T's, the stripe count. With three stripes and one megabyte chunks, chunk one goes to the first target, two to the second, three to the third, and then four wraps back to the first.",
 "Each O S T holds one object for the file, so the first target's object contains chunks one, four, seven, and so on, packed together. The layout is stored with the file on the metadata server.",
 "You choose these per file, or per directory so new files inherit them. The command is L F S setstripe. The default stripe count is one: a new file lives on a single target. That suits small files and wastes a huge shared one. Minus one means use all the targets, and the maximum is two thousand.",
 "Here's the idealized payoff. Every extra stripe adds a server's worth of bandwidth, until something else saturates, usually the client's own network link. This curve is a model, with assumed per-target and per-client numbers, to show the shape. It is not a benchmark.",
 "Striping has a second job: balance. Suppose thousands of files each land on random targets. With stripe count one, bad luck leaves some targets much fuller than others. I simulated that. With the same total data, the fullest target holds almost four times the average, and spreading each file across more targets shrinks the gap. Real allocators steer new files toward emptier targets, so this shows the problem they solve, not what you'd observe.",
 "But more stripes isn't free. Each extra stripe is another server to contact, another lock, and another failure that can take the file offline. That's why the default is small, big shared files get wide layouts, and small files stay narrow.",
]),
("s5", [
 "Striping buys performance. To keep it correct, Lustre needs locks. The Lustre Distributed Lock Manager, the L D L M, makes sure that when one client writes and another reads, the reader sees the data, the way it would on a local disk.",
 "Each lock is managed by a single server, and in the two thousand three design it never migrates between masters. Locks on a file's data are managed by the storage servers that hold its objects. The paper makes a point of how small this is: the whole lock manager was just over four thousand lines of code.",
 "Data locks cover bite ranges. And here's a trick that should sound familiar. The server may grant a lock larger than the one requested. If you're the only client touching a file, you typically get a lock on the whole file, and never ask again. When a conflicting request arrives, the server calls the holder back, and the ranges are renegotiated.",
 "Metadata has its own trick: intent locks. Creating a file would normally take several steps: look up the directory, take a lock, then create. With intent locking, the client sends its whole intention, open or create, in one request. The metadata server performs it, and returns the result together with a lock if one is useful.",
 "The two thousand three paper also describes choosing by contention. In a quiet directory, hand the client a lock so it can cache and batch changes, though the paper says that cache was still planned. In a hot directory, like slash tmp, grant no lock at all. The server just does each operation itself, because passing a lock back and forth would cost more than the work.",
 "Compare with G P F S. Both use bite-range locks with generous grants that shrink under contention. The real difference is where the lock state lives: spread across token managers in G P F S, held by the server that owns the object in Lustre.",
]),
("s6", [
 "Everything so far talks over a network, and Lustre has its own layer for that: L Net. It gives the file system one messaging interface, and underneath, drivers called L N D's for each fabric: T C P over Ethernet, InfiniBand, Omni-Path, Rocky, and several proprietary ones.",
 "Every node has an address of the form: address at network type, called a N I D. Servers listen on T C P port nine eighty eight. L Net was originally based on the Portals interface from Sandia, and it uses remote direct memory access where the hardware allows, so bulk data moves without the processor copying it.",
 "Networks don't always match, so L Net routers bridge one network to another. Frontier's file system, which we'll meet shortly, has a hundred sixty nine of them. And since version two point ten, multi-rail lets one node bundle several network interfaces into a fatter pipe.",
 "Bulk transfers have grown. The largest request was four megabytes in version two point four, sixteen in two point nine, and up to sixty four megabytes in two point thirteen. My reading: the same story as block sizes. As disks and networks got faster, bigger requests were needed to amortize the fixed cost of each one.",
 "For perspective, in the two thousand three paper, two nodes over a Quadrics Elan network moved over three hundred megabytes per second, limited by the P C I bus. And one shared file scaled across eleven hundred nodes, the limit of the hardware they had. That was the frontier then.",
]),
("s7", [
 "Back to the trade-off we flagged. With one metadata server, every create, stat, and rename in the whole system funnels through a single machine. Lustre has attacked that bottleneck in layers.",
 "First, the Distributed Namespace Environment, D N E. Version two point four, in twenty thirteen, let subdirectory trees live on different metadata targets. Later versions can spread one huge directory across targets, and the two point fifteen defaults can place new directories round-robin or by free space.",
 "Second, small files. Opening and reading a small file normally costs a trip to the metadata server and then a trip to a storage server. Data on M D T, from version two point eleven, stores the first piece of a file right on the metadata target, which helps small files.",
 "Third, layouts that change as files grow. Progressive file layouts, from version two point ten, let one file use different layouts for different regions: narrow at the start, wider as it grows. File level redundancy, a year later, added mirrored layouts for availability and read performance.",
 "Under all of it, targets are formatted with a local file system. Originally that was L disk F S, a modified ext four. Since version two point four, Z F S is supported too, with a much higher maximum file size: eight exbibytes, against about thirty one pebibytes for L disk F S.",
 "Failures are handled in pairs: a partner server can mount a dead server's targets. A client that goes silent is evicted after a timeout, by default a hundred seconds. Its locks are revoked and its unwritten cached data is discarded. Eviction is the price of letting everyone else keep going.",
 "The documented limits show how far this has stretched. The two thousand three paper's goal was tens of thousands of clients. Today's manual lists up to eight thousand one hundred fifty targets, two hundred fifty six metadata targets, and a hundred thirty one thousand clients, with more than thirty thousand tested in production.",
]),
("s8", [
 "Now the real thing. Frontier, at Oak Ridge, was number one on the Top five hundred in twenty twenty two. Its file system is called Orion: Lustre on top of Z F S, on HPE ClusterStor hardware. Oak Ridge describes it as roughly seven hundred petabytes in total.",
 "It has three tiers. A metadata tier of N V M E flash: four hundred eighty drives, about ten petabytes. A performance tier of flash: fifty four hundred drives, eleven and a half petabytes. And a capacity tier: forty seven thousand seven hundred hard disks, six hundred seventy nine petabytes.",
 "The server layout, from a Lustre User Group talk: two management servers, forty metadata servers, four hundred fifty object storage servers, and a hundred sixty nine L Net routers. Within each enclosure, drives are grouped by Z F S declustered RAID, the same declustering idea we met with G P F S.",
 "The cleverest part is the default layout, a progressive file layout. The first two hundred fifty six kilobytes of every file live on the metadata flash. Up to eight megabytes goes to one flash target. Beyond that, to disk, a single stripe up to one hundred twenty eight gigabytes. And past that, eight stripes across disks.",
 "So a file starts on the fastest storage and moves outward through the tiers as it grows, with no action from the user. A file under eight megabytes never touches a hard disk. It's an architecture decision, not a hardware trick: one namespace, different layout rules by size.",
 "Oak Ridge lists about ten terabytes per second for the flash tier. For the disk tier, about five; two Oak Ridge sources disagree about whether reads or writes are faster, so I'll just say about five. Compare the G P F S machine from the two thousand two paper: roughly five thousand times the capacity, and on flash, eight hundred times the bandwidth. Different machines, different decades. Direction only.",
]),
("s9", [
 "So how do the two designs compare? Start with what they share. Both stripe files across many servers. Both offer POSIX semantics through bite-range locking. Both put metadata on flash these days. Both run machines at the top of the Top five hundred.",
 "Where they differ is who owns the disks. In G P F S, any node can reach any disk, coordination is by tokens, and a failed node's log is replayed by another. In Lustre, each target is served by one server at a time, clients are strictly clients, and coordination is between a client and the server that owns the thing.",
 "That's why Lustre's metadata scaling is a feature you add, D N E, while G P F S distributes metadata work across nodes by design. It's also why Lustre's data path is easy to reason about: client, network, server, disk.",
 "Neither is better in general. This comparison comes from architecture, not benchmarks, and I haven't seen a fair benchmark of the two on identical hardware. Be suspicious of anyone who says one is simply faster.",
 "Where do they fit? Above them, burst buffers and node-local flash. Below them, archives and tape. They are the big, shared, always-on middle of that stack, and the layers around them are the natural next thing to study.",
 "To recap. Lustre separates metadata from data. A file is a layout of objects on storage targets. The client gets the layout once, then talks to the servers directly. Locks keep it coherent, L Net moves the bytes, and D N E, progressive layouts, and data on M D T keep stretching the design. The sources are on screen, and the code that made this video is in the package.",
]),
]
